WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.389
Brute force is simple, exact, and linear.

2
00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:11.011
The top_k you wrote in lesson 1 is exact (or brute-force, or flat) search: score the query against every vector, keep the best k.

3
00:00:11.291 --> 00:00:12.976
Its cost is easy to state.

4
00:00:13.256 --> 00:00:20.317
For n vectors of dimension d, each query takes n times d multiply-adds and reads every vector from memory.

5
00:00:20.597 --> 00:00:31.755
At 10 million vectors of 1,024 dimensions, that’s about 10 billion multiply-adds per query and roughly 41 GB of float32 vectors to stream through.

6
00:00:32.035 --> 00:00:37.987
Before you reach for anything cleverer, run the numbers for your own case, because brute force is often enough.

7
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:55.397
A help center with 50,000 chunks at 1,024 dimensions is about 200 MB of vectors and around 50 million multiply-adds per query, well within what an optimized matrix library does on one CPU in a few milliseconds.

8
00:00:55.677 --> 00:01:13.896
The Faiss documentation describes its flat index as the only index that guarantees exact results, recommends it as the baseline every other index is measured against, and notes it is the best choice when you’ll run only a modest number of searches, since building an index won’t pay for itself.

9
00:01:14.176 --> 00:01:20.384
pgvector, the Postgres extension, does exact search by default and calls that perfect recall.

10
00:01:20.664 --> 00:01:29.261
Exact search stops being enough when the corpus reaches millions of vectors, when you need many queries per second, or when the vectors don’t fit in memory.

11
00:01:29.541 --> 00:01:32.379
Then you trade a little accuracy for a lot of speed.
